1
00:00:00,226 --> 00:00:54,610
Low-intensity isometric holds are a new hot topic in the arm wrestling community that everyone is talking about, but I believe the discussion lacks depth. I mean, what are we saying about it? Because it's not magic; it has some dependencies. And as a person who implemented this kind of training over two years ago in my routine and has recommended it the whole time to a bunch of people who used it with great success, I'm going to share my opinion. Also, we're going to look a little deeper into the studies, so you know exactly how and what it's about, because it's not exactly like, "Do this and you're going to have steel tendons." That's

2
00:00:54,786 --> 00:00:56,048
an oversimplification.

3
00:00:57,830 --> 00:01:11,007
So first, I'm going to refer to Keith Baar's studies because he's probably the person whose name is used most often in this discussion.

4
00:01:11,708 --> 00:01:22,482
So his discovery, actually his study, that we are using or referring to was

5
00:01:22,690 --> 00:01:24,652
this one you see on the screen right now.

6
00:01:24,692 --> 00:01:32,201
Ten-minute continuous loading with six hours of rest improved tendon stiffness.

7
00:01:34,483 --> 00:01:41,832
Actually, this isn't really true, but this is taken from another thing.

8
00:01:42,032 --> 00:01:43,994
That's why I put it like that.

9
00:01:44,034 --> 00:01:45,576
But in general, that was it.

10
00:01:45,696 --> 00:01:50,962
So for ten minutes, an artificial ligament,

11
00:01:51,234 --> 00:01:56,401
an artificial ligament produced in a lab was tested and showed some results.

12
00:01:58,024 --> 00:02:17,711
So the first thing we need to understand for practical use, that we can take from this study, is that it actually involved about three hundred cycles, each two seconds long, of stretching and shortening the artificial ligament.

13
00:02:18,833 --> 00:02:19,634
So it's not

14
00:02:19,746 --> 00:02:37,965
what they did in the Abrahamsson protocol that we're going to discuss later on, because probably this is also why the Keith Baar study is so popular: it was tested by Emil Abrahamsson, and it showed a great strength increase in his case.

15
00:02:39,066 --> 00:02:42,509
But we'll talk a bit later about that in this video.

16
00:02:42,529 --> 00:02:47,154
So first of all, as I said, it was like ten minutes of continuous

17
00:02:47,362 --> 00:02:52,368
stretching and shortening of the artificial ligament, which we don't do.

18
00:02:53,649 --> 00:03:04,842
Second thing is that this ligament was stretched to a certain level, to a certain length, and this length was the same every time.

19
00:03:05,843 --> 00:03:12,531
And that's the biggest problem with implementing Keith Baar's studies into real life.

20
00:03:12,551 --> 00:03:15,634
Because if we're going to take, like, literally these ten minutes,

21
00:03:15,906 --> 00:03:41,554
literally six hours and everything, you're going to end up with something that you absolutely cannot... It's simply impossible to recover from it if you're going to do it three times a day, for example. So what can we take then? Because why am I saying, like, if you stretch the tendon or ligament to a certain

22
00:03:41,986 --> 00:03:45,771
length, why can't we use this information?

23
00:03:45,831 --> 00:03:47,834
It's very simple. How much, okay,

24
00:03:47,874 --> 00:03:52,000
I'm doing holds, how much are my ligaments, how much is my tendon stretched?

25
00:03:53,643 --> 00:04:10,066
We don't know that. You can't really measure it unless you have, like, a proper three-dimensional model of it. But it's also very person-specific. There is some information later on in this PDF that actually the same loading

26
00:04:10,850 --> 00:04:19,359
can lead to almost a twofold difference in tendon stretch because it depends on tendon stiffness.

27
00:04:19,479 --> 00:04:27,647
So in general, what is stiffness? Because that's also worth mentioning: stiffness is resistance to stretching.

28
00:04:28,107 --> 00:04:35,114
So the more force you have to put into stretching to stretch it by one millimeter, that means the tendon is stiffer.

29
00:04:35,655 --> 00:04:38,738
A tendon is elastic, not when it's not stiff,

30
00:04:39,234 --> 00:05:08,690
but when it can get longer without rupturing. So something can be stiff and elastic at the same time. Tendons, so, so that's a funny thing. On the surface, it can look a little bit contradictory, but it's not. So what can we really take from Keith Baar's study, a study made in a lab? We can take two things. So the first thing is that

31
00:05:09,730 --> 00:05:18,279
additional loading during a window doesn't necessarily have to be beneficial.

32
00:05:18,860 --> 00:05:27,849
So there is a window after which you cannot, or the benefit from work is low, okay?

33
00:05:28,910 --> 00:05:36,178
What we take as the second thing is there is an idle time that has to be spent to

34
00:05:36,834 --> 00:05:43,063
reset this cell, this tissue, to listen to the stimulus again.

35
00:05:43,424 --> 00:05:56,723
So these are the two things because, unfortunately, the other things are not really important for us because we cannot do it at the same level of control as they did in the laboratory.

36
00:05:56,743 --> 00:06:04,434
So this is the first thing for practical use you can take from

37
00:06:04,706 --> 00:06:10,752
Keith Baar, and we're going to go straight to Abrahamsson, okay?

38
00:06:11,173 --> 00:06:13,215
Because I want to discuss it in this order.

39
00:06:14,556 --> 00:06:19,561
So Emil Abrahamsson saw this, I mean, his brother, but it doesn't matter.

40
00:06:20,122 --> 00:06:31,954
They decided to test this protocol, and during one month he increased his finger strength by thirty percent in a one-rep max. Crazy amount.

41
00:06:32,066 --> 00:06:52,366
And later on, they got in touch with Keith Baar and did a retrospective study where he actually posted his protocol, and they already had data for the people who used this protocol and could track the results.

42
00:06:52,386 --> 00:06:53,827
But this is a retrospective study.

43
00:06:53,848 --> 00:06:56,410
So they took the data and built a study upon it.

44
00:06:56,690 --> 00:07:00,434
There are always, like, you know,

45
00:07:00,706 --> 00:07:26,194
lower-quality studies made like that, but they didn't have a choice. But what is interesting, and what is really interesting for us in practical implementation, is that the guys who were doing this Abrahamsson, right, so only low-intensity isometric training, had increasing strength. Those guys who were doing max hangs, so classic, like

46
00:07:26,274 --> 00:07:29,557
the normal way of getting stronger, the classical way of getting stronger.

47
00:07:29,577 --> 00:07:37,084
So hard, heavy, high-intensity singles also got stronger.

48
00:07:37,825 --> 00:07:47,674
But those who were doing both almost doubled the effect.

49
00:07:47,694 --> 00:07:52,019
So we're taking the third thing from this study.

50
00:07:52,159 --> 00:07:55,602
So those two

51
00:07:56,290 --> 00:08:26,226
ways of training, so strength training and the Abrahamsson method, or the low-intensity isometrics method, work and get your tendons, get your strength to a different level, but through different mechanisms. So it's not the same mechanism that overlaps. So you can stack them one onto another. So this is the third thing we can take from this, okay?

52
00:08:26,306 --> 00:08:28,770
