55
00:08:40,168 --> 00:08:46,658
And this is something you can do, but in my opinion, we need to consider many factors.

56
00:08:46,678 --> 00:08:48,441
So he used that for the fingers.

57
00:08:49,402 --> 00:08:54,290
So the tendons of the fingers are insanely long compared to the other ones.

58
00:08:54,722 --> 00:09:02,297
So the question is whether the biceps will react the same way, or something like, I don't know, the pronator teres, which has a very short tendon, right?

59
00:09:03,519 --> 00:09:04,822
Compared to fingers, obviously.

60
00:09:05,864 --> 00:09:15,122
So this is not measured, and this is not... I cannot, after using this protocol for two years, I cannot give you...

61
00:09:15,906 --> 00:09:45,394
one answer because obviously I'm too small, you know, a control group of one person. I cannot definitely tell you that, okay, you're going to do this and you're going to get this, as Emil Abrahamsson, you're going to get the same result in strength, or even as the climbers from the protocols they tested. I cannot guarantee you that because I have not seen that in my case yet. I'm doing that for two years, and I'm going to tell you, I'll tell you why. So obviously this is very useful.

62
00:09:45,794 --> 00:09:49,518
If you want to test it, it was tested for fingers many times.

63
00:09:49,578 --> 00:10:08,837
If you want to stop training altogether and test the Abrahamsson method, measure everything for a month or two and then come back, yeah, just go to the comment section and tell me if you did this experiment for your other muscles or how much it improved your strength gains.

64
00:10:08,917 --> 00:10:14,322
But I think that the real deal about these low-intensity isometrics

65
00:10:15,298 --> 00:10:43,826
lies somewhere else, because if we go to an original study like this, this one, because this was the first one that gave, like, a very, very huge improvement in pain relief after injury, they had this protocol: five times forty-five seconds for knee extension. So they measured, like, voluntary

66
00:10:43,906 --> 00:11:12,466
MVC, maximum voluntary contraction, for the injured place, and then they did it at, like, seventy percent of it, if I remember well. I think so, yes. Anyway, it doesn't really matter, but they loaded it five times for forty-five seconds and they had a great reduction in pain. But in pain, they didn't measure anything else outside of pain.

67
00:11:12,770 --> 00:11:37,010
And they tried to redo this study because there were only six guys in this study. They tried to redo it with a bigger group, and they found it's very person-specific. For one, it was really important and it really helped reduce pain; for the other, not. But in general, from my experience, because I'm recommending this stuff, like

68
00:11:37,218 --> 00:11:42,103
to everyone who has any issues or is new to arm wrestling, whatever.

69
00:11:42,563 --> 00:11:51,151
